    },
    {
      "id": "00a57ce1-5080-4fd1-9a2d-0365669e1427",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 516,
      "y": 206,
      "width": 932.64,
      "height": 162.0,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e293b",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 42,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "PARAMETER                      SN1 (Unimolecular Substitution)                    SN2 (Bimolecular Substitution)\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKinetics & Rate Law            Rate = k[R-X] (1st order, independent of [Nu-])    Rate = k[R-X][Nu-] (2nd order, bimolecular)\nSteps & Intermediates          Two-step process via planar carbocation (sp2)      Single-step concerted via pentacoordinate T.S.\nStereochemical Outcome         Racemisation (with slight net inversion)           Complete Walden Inversion (100% inverted)\nSubstrate Reactivity           3\u00b0 > 2\u00b0 > 1\u00b0 > CH3-X (Allylic & Benzylic >> 3\u00b0)    CH3-X > 1\u00b0 > 2\u00b0 > 3\u00b0 (Steric hindrance governs)\nNucleophile Strength           Weak nucleophiles sufficient (H2O, EtOH, AcOH)     Strong nucleophiles required (OH-, RO-, CN-, I-)\nSolvent Preference             Polar Protic solvents (H2O, EtOH) stabilize ions   Polar Aprotic solvents (Acetone, DMSO, DMF)\nLeaving Group nucleofugality   I- > Br- > Cl- >> F- (Controlled by C-X strength)  I- > Br- > Cl- >> F- (Inversely related to basicity)\nRearrangement Potential        Frequent 1,2-hydride and 1,2-methyl shifts         Strictly absent (concerted displacement)",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 12,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "PARAMETER                      SN1 (Unimolecular Substitution)                    SN2 (Bimolecular Substitution)\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKinetics & Rate Law            Rate = k[R-X] (1st order, independent of [Nu-])    Rate = k[R-X][Nu-] (2nd order, bimolecular)\nSteps & Intermediates          Two-step process via planar carbocation (sp2)      Single-step concerted via pentacoordinate T.S.\nStereochemical Outcome         Racemisation (with slight net inversion)           Complete Walden Inversion (100% inverted)\nSubstrate Reactivity           3\u00b0 > 2\u00b0 > 1\u00b0 > CH3-X (Allylic & Benzylic >> 3\u00b0)    CH3-X > 1\u00b0 > 2\u00b0 > 3\u00b0 (Steric hindrance governs)\nNucleophile Strength           Weak nucleophiles sufficient (H2O, EtOH, AcOH)     Strong nucleophiles required (OH-, RO-, CN-, I-)\nSolvent Preference             Polar Protic solvents (H2O, EtOH) stabilize ions   Polar Aprotic solvents (Acetone, DMSO, DMF)\nLeaving Group nucleofugality   I- > Br- > Cl- >> F- (Controlled by C-X strength)  I- > Br- > Cl- >> F- (Inversely related to basicity)\nRearrangement Potential        Frequent 1,2-hydride and 1,2-methyl shifts         Strictly absent (concerted displacement)",
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
