      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "NCERT CANONICAL",
      "fontSize": 11,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 11,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "NCERT CANONICAL",
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "cf2df46f-8006-43d0-90fa-e11efff30576",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2066,
      "y": 206,
      "width": 981.36,
      "height": 194.4,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e293b",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 42,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "PARAMETER                      E1 ELIMINATION                                     E2 ELIMINATION\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMechanism Type                 Two-step: Carbocation formation -> deprotonation   Concerted single-step: Anti-periplanar beta-H abstraction\nRate Law                       Rate = k[R-X] (1st Order unimolecular)             Rate = k[R-X][Base] (2nd Order bimolecular)\nBase Requirement               Weak base, high temperature (H2O, EtOH / heat)     Strong bulky or unhindered base (alc. KOH, EtO-Na+)\nGeometry Requirement           No strict stereospecific requirement               Strict requirement: H-C-C-X must be Anti-Periplanar (180\u00b0)\nSaytzeff (Zaitsev) Rule:       Major product is the more substituted, highly alkylated alkene (thermodynamically favored stability).\n                               Governed by greater hyperconjugation structures (> number of alpha-hydrogens) and lower heat of hydrogenation.\nHofmann Elimination Rule:      Major product is the less substituted (terminal) alkene when:\n                               1. Leaving group is poor (Fluoride, -NR3+, -SR2+). Carbon bearing H develops substantial carbanion character.\n                               2. Base is sterically hindered / bulky (Potassium tert-butoxide, t-BuO-K+; (i-Pr)2N-Li+ LDA).\nRegioselectivity Shift:        2-Bromobutane + alc. KOH -> 2-Butene (Saytzeff 81%) vs 2-Bromobutane + t-BuOK -> 1-Butene (Hofmann 72%)",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 12,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "PARAMETER                      E1 ELIMINATION                                     E2 ELIMINATION\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMechanism Type                 Two-step: Carbocation formation -> deprotonation   Concerted single-step: Anti-periplanar beta-H abstraction\nRate Law                       Rate = k[R-X] (1st Order unimolecular)             Rate = k[R-X][Base] (2nd Order bimolecular)\nBase Requirement               Weak base, high temperature (H2O, EtOH / heat)     Strong bulky or unhindered base (alc. KOH, EtO-Na+)\nGeometry Requirement           No strict stereospecific requirement               Strict requirement: H-C-C-X must be Anti-Periplanar (180\u00b0)\nSaytzeff (Zaitsev) Rule:       Major product is the more substituted, highly alkylated alkene (thermodynamically favored stability).\n                               Governed by greater hyperconjugation structures (> number of alpha-hydrogens) and lower heat of hydrogenation.\nHofmann Elimination Rule:      Major product is the less substituted (terminal) alkene when:\n                               1. Leaving group is poor (Fluoride, -NR3+, -SR2+). Carbon bearing H develops substantial carbanion character.\n                               2. Base is sterically hindered / bulky (Potassium tert-butoxide, t-BuO-K+; (i-Pr)2N-Li+ LDA).\nRegioselectivity Shift:        2-Bromobutane + alc. KOH -> 2-Butene (Saytzeff 81%) vs 2-Bromobutane + t-BuOK -> 1-Butene (Hofmann 72%)",
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
