    },
    {
      "id": "64d64436-9d54-46af-9ff5-1ea8cc9a1cf7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3616,
      "y": 206,
      "width": 1037.04,
      "height": 178.2,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e293b",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 42,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "PHYSICAL PROPERTY              HALOALKANE TRENDS & ANOMALIES                      EXPLANATION & NCERT EXPERIMENTAL DATA\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nC-X Bond Length                CH3-F (139 pm) < CH3-Cl (178 pm) < CH3-Br (193 pm) < CH3-I (214 pm) [Due to increasing halogen atomic radius]\nC-X Bond Enthalpy (kJ/mol)     CH3-F (452) > CH3-Cl (351) > CH3-Br (293) > CH3-I (234) [Decreases down group; determines C-X chemical reactivity]\nDipole Moment Anomaly          CH3-Cl (1.860 D) > CH3-F (1.847 D) > CH3-Br (1.830 D) > CH3-I (1.636 D) [Dipole = q x d; C-F bond length is tiny!]\nBoiling Points (Same Alkyl)    R-I > R-Br > R-Cl > R-F [Directly proportional to molecular mass and polarizability of electron cloud (van der Waals)]\nIsomeric Haloalkanes BP        Primary (1\u00b0) > Secondary (2\u00b0) > Tertiary (3\u00b0) [Branching makes molecule spherical, reduces surface area -> lower BP]\nDichlorobenzene BP & MP        Boiling points are nearly identical: ortho (453 K), meta (446 K), para (448 K).\n                               Melting point anomaly: para-isomer (325 K) >> ortho (256 K), meta (249 K) [p-isomer packs symmetrically in crystal!]\nSolubility in Water            Extremely low (immiscible), despite polarity. Energy released from haloalkane-H2O attraction cannot break H2O H-bonds.\nSolubility in Organics         Highly soluble in non-polar organic solvents (benzene, diethyl ether, carbon tetrachloride, petroleum ether).",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 12,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "PHYSICAL PROPERTY              HALOALKANE TRENDS & ANOMALIES                      EXPLANATION & NCERT EXPERIMENTAL DATA\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nC-X Bond Length                CH3-F (139 pm) < CH3-Cl (178 pm) < CH3-Br (193 pm) < CH3-I (214 pm) [Due to increasing halogen atomic radius]\nC-X Bond Enthalpy (kJ/mol)     CH3-F (452) > CH3-Cl (351) > CH3-Br (293) > CH3-I (234) [Decreases down group; determines C-X chemical reactivity]\nDipole Moment Anomaly          CH3-Cl (1.860 D) > CH3-F (1.847 D) > CH3-Br (1.830 D) > CH3-I (1.636 D) [Dipole = q x d; C-F bond length is tiny!]\nBoiling Points (Same Alkyl)    R-I > R-Br > R-Cl > R-F [Directly proportional to molecular mass and polarizability of electron cloud (van der Waals)]\nIsomeric Haloalkanes BP        Primary (1\u00b0) > Secondary (2\u00b0) > Tertiary (3\u00b0) [Branching makes molecule spherical, reduces surface area -> lower BP]\nDichlorobenzene BP & MP        Boiling points are nearly identical: ortho (453 K), meta (446 K), para (448 K).\n                               Melting point anomaly: para-isomer (325 K) >> ortho (256 K), meta (249 K) [p-isomer packs symmetrically in crystal!]\nSolubility in Water            Extremely low (immiscible), despite polarity. Energy released from haloalkane-H2O attraction cannot break H2O H-bonds.\nSolubility in Organics         Highly soluble in non-polar organic solvents (benzene, diethyl ether, carbon tetrachloride, petroleum ether).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
