    {
      "id": "ad686a1f-9e10-497b-99a9-d909c7d94399",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5166,
      "y": 206,
      "width": 1050.96,
      "height": 194.4,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e293b",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 42,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "CONCEPT                        DEFINING PRINCIPLE                                 APPLICATION TO HALOALKANES\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChirality & Asymmetric C       Carbon bonded to 4 distinctly different ligands;   e.g. Butan-2-ol, 2-Chlorobutane, 1-Bromo-1-chloroethane.\n                               Molecule is non-superimposable on mirror image.    Possesses molecular dissymmetry; rotates plane-polarized light.\nEnantiomers                    Non-superimposable stereoisomers (mirror images);  Identical physical properties (BP, MP, refractive index, density);\n                               Equal and opposite optical rotation (+d / -l).     Differ ONLY in sign of specific optical rotation and enzyme action.\nRacemic Mixture (\u00b1)            1:1 Equimolar mixture of enantiomers (+ and -);    Optical activity is exactly zero due to EXTERNAL COMPENSATION.\n                               Formed during pure SN1 solvolysis.                 Can be resolved into optically active enantiomers by chiral reagents.\nDiastereomers                  Stereoisomers that are NOT mirror images.          Possess different physical properties (BP, MP, solubilities, etc.).\nMeso Compounds                 Molecules with stereocenters + internal symmetry.  Optically inactive due to INTERNAL COMPENSATION (e.g. meso-tartaric).\nRetention vs Inversion         Retention: Spatial configuration unchanged (SNi).  Inversion: Umbrella flipping 100% backside attack (SN2).\nRacemisation Pathway           Planar carbocation attacked with equal probability from front and rear face (50% retention + 50% inversion).",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 12,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "CONCEPT                        DEFINING PRINCIPLE                                 APPLICATION TO HALOALKANES\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChirality & Asymmetric C       Carbon bonded to 4 distinctly different ligands;   e.g. Butan-2-ol, 2-Chlorobutane, 1-Bromo-1-chloroethane.\n                               Molecule is non-superimposable on mirror image.    Possesses molecular dissymmetry; rotates plane-polarized light.\nEnantiomers                    Non-superimposable stereoisomers (mirror images);  Identical physical properties (BP, MP, refractive index, density);\n                               Equal and opposite optical rotation (+d / -l).     Differ ONLY in sign of specific optical rotation and enzyme action.\nRacemic Mixture (\u00b1)            1:1 Equimolar mixture of enantiomers (+ and -);    Optical activity is exactly zero due to EXTERNAL COMPENSATION.\n                               Formed during pure SN1 solvolysis.                 Can be resolved into optically active enantiomers by chiral reagents.\nDiastereomers                  Stereoisomers that are NOT mirror images.          Possess different physical properties (BP, MP, solubilities, etc.).\nMeso Compounds                 Molecules with stereocenters + internal symmetry.  Optically inactive due to INTERNAL COMPENSATION (e.g. meso-tartaric).\nRetention vs Inversion         Retention: Spatial configuration unchanged (SNi).  Inversion: Umbrella flipping 100% backside attack (SN2).\nRacemisation Pathway           Planar carbocation attacked with equal probability from front and rear face (50% retention + 50% inversion).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
