      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "NCERT CLASSIC",
      "fontSize": 11,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 11,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "NCERT CLASSIC",
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "7e2f0a7a-6246-47ad-acd3-05e57624411b",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 366,
      "y": 2226,
      "width": 786.48,
      "height": 162.0,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e293b",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 42,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "AMBIDENT NUCLEOPHILES DUAL REACTIVITY PRINCIPLE (NCERT TABLE 6.4)\n\u2022 Ambident Nucleophile: Nucleophiles possessing two distinct donor nucleophilic centers (C vs N or O vs N).\n\u2022 Case 1: Cyanide Ion (:C#N: <-> :C=N:-)\n  - KCN is primarily IONIC: Both C and N can donate electrons. C-attack predominates because C-C bond energy\n    (348 kJ/mol) is significantly higher than C-N bond energy (305 kJ/mol) -> Yields Alkane Nitrile (R-CN).\n  - AgCN is primarily COVALENT: Ag-C bond is covalent; C lone pair is shielded. Nitrogen lone pair attacks ->\n    Yields Alkyl Isocyanide / Carbylamine (R-NC).\n\u2022 Case 2: Nitrite Ion (-O-N=O <-> O=N-O-)\n  - KNO2 is primarily IONIC: Negative charge resides on oxygen -> O-attack -> Yields Alkyl Nitrite (R-O-N=O).\n  - AgNO2 is primarily COVALENT: Ag-O bond is covalent; Nitrogen lone pair attacks -> Yields Nitroalkane (R-NO2).",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 12,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "AMBIDENT NUCLEOPHILES DUAL REACTIVITY PRINCIPLE (NCERT TABLE 6.4)\n\u2022 Ambident Nucleophile: Nucleophiles possessing two distinct donor nucleophilic centers (C vs N or O vs N).\n\u2022 Case 1: Cyanide Ion (:C#N: <-> :C=N:-)\n  - KCN is primarily IONIC: Both C and N can donate electrons. C-attack predominates because C-C bond energy\n    (348 kJ/mol) is significantly higher than C-N bond energy (305 kJ/mol) -> Yields Alkane Nitrile (R-CN).\n  - AgCN is primarily COVALENT: Ag-C bond is covalent; C lone pair is shielded. Nitrogen lone pair attacks ->\n    Yields Alkyl Isocyanide / Carbylamine (R-NC).\n\u2022 Case 2: Nitrite Ion (-O-N=O <-> O=N-O-)\n  - KNO2 is primarily IONIC: Negative charge resides on oxygen -> O-attack -> Yields Alkyl Nitrite (R-O-N=O).\n  - AgNO2 is primarily COVALENT: Ag-O bond is covalent; Nitrogen lone pair attacks -> Yields Nitroalkane (R-NO2).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
