    {
      "id": "3cfec334-6058-495c-88a3-238431307249",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2196,
      "y": 2726,
      "width": 723.84,
      "height": 113.4,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e293b",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 42,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "REMARKABLE INERTNESS TOWARDS NUCLEOPHILIC AROMATIC SUBSTITUTION:\n1. Resonance Effect (+R): Delocalization of Cl lone pairs gives partial double bond character\n   to C-Cl bond. Bond length is shortened to 169 pm (vs 177 pm in alkyl chloride), resisting cleavage.\n2. Hybridization Difference: Carbon is sp2 hybridized (33.3% s-character) -> more electronegative,\n   holding shared C-Cl electron pair much more tightly than sp3 carbon (25% s-character) in haloalkane.\n3. Phenyl Cation Instability: Phenyl cation formed by self-ionization cannot be stabilized by resonance.\n4. Pi-Electron Repulsion: Electron-rich incoming nucleophile is repelled by aromatic pi-cloud.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 12,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "REMARKABLE INERTNESS TOWARDS NUCLEOPHILIC AROMATIC SUBSTITUTION:\n1. Resonance Effect (+R): Delocalization of Cl lone pairs gives partial double bond character\n   to C-Cl bond. Bond length is shortened to 169 pm (vs 177 pm in alkyl chloride), resisting cleavage.\n2. Hybridization Difference: Carbon is sp2 hybridized (33.3% s-character) -> more electronegative,\n   holding shared C-Cl electron pair much more tightly than sp3 carbon (25% s-character) in haloalkane.\n3. Phenyl Cation Instability: Phenyl cation formed by self-ionization cannot be stabilized by resonance.\n4. Pi-Electron Repulsion: Electron-rich incoming nucleophile is repelled by aromatic pi-cloud.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
