      "text": "MASTER REVISION CHEAT-SHEET \u2022 10 NON-NEGOTIABLE NCERT EXAM LAWS:\n1. Reactivity Order towards SN1: Benzylic > Allylic > 3\u00b0 > 2\u00b0 > 1\u00b0 > CH3-X (Stabilized by carbocation resonance or hyperconjugation).\n2. Reactivity Order towards SN2: CH3-X > 1\u00b0 > 2\u00b0 > 3\u00b0 (Strictly steric hindrance governed; 3\u00b0 halides virtually never undergo SN2).\n3. Dipole Moment Hierarchy: CH3Cl (1.860 D) > CH3F (1.847 D) > CH3Br (1.830 D) > CH3I (1.636 D) [Anomaly: C-F bond length is tiny].\n4. Boiling Point Hierarchy: R-I > R-Br > R-Cl > R-F (van der Waals dispersion forces increase with molecular mass and polarizability).\n5. Melting Point of Dichlorobenzenes: Para >> Ortho > Meta (Para isomer has symmetrical molecular geometry that fits tightly into crystal lattice).\n6. Ambident Nucleophiles: KCN yields R-CN (ionic, C-C bond energy is higher); AgCN yields R-NC (covalent, N-attack). KNO2 yields R-ONO; AgNO2 yields R-NO2.\n7. Low Reactivity of Chlorobenzene: (a) Partial double bond character (+R resonance), (b) sp2 carbon higher electronegativity, (c) phenyl cation instability, (d) pi-cloud repulsion.\n8. Directing Effect in Haloarenes: Halogen is ORTHO/PARA directing (+R resonance effect), but DEACTIVATING (-I inductive effect dominates arenium stabilization).\n9. Grignard Anhydrous Mandate: RMgX reacts instantly with any active hydrogen compound (H2O, ROH, RNH2) to form alkane R-H. Even moisture destroys reagent.\n10. Williamson Ether Limitation: 3\u00b0 alkyl halide + alkoxide yields 100% alkene via E2 elimination; 1\u00b0 alkyl halide is required to obtain ether.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 12,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "MASTER REVISION CHEAT-SHEET \u2022 10 NON-NEGOTIABLE NCERT EXAM LAWS:\n1. Reactivity Order towards SN1: Benzylic > Allylic > 3\u00b0 > 2\u00b0 > 1\u00b0 > CH3-X (Stabilized by carbocation resonance or hyperconjugation).\n2. Reactivity Order towards SN2: CH3-X > 1\u00b0 > 2\u00b0 > 3\u00b0 (Strictly steric hindrance governed; 3\u00b0 halides virtually never undergo SN2).\n3. Dipole Moment Hierarchy: CH3Cl (1.860 D) > CH3F (1.847 D) > CH3Br (1.830 D) > CH3I (1.636 D) [Anomaly: C-F bond length is tiny].\n4. Boiling Point Hierarchy: R-I > R-Br > R-Cl > R-F (van der Waals dispersion forces increase with molecular mass and polarizability).\n5. Melting Point of Dichlorobenzenes: Para >> Ortho > Meta (Para isomer has symmetrical molecular geometry that fits tightly into crystal lattice).\n6. Ambident Nucleophiles: KCN yields R-CN (ionic, C-C bond energy is higher); AgCN yields R-NC (covalent, N-attack). KNO2 yields R-ONO; AgNO2 yields R-NO2.\n7. Low Reactivity of Chlorobenzene: (a) Partial double bond character (+R resonance), (b) sp2 carbon higher electronegativity, (c) phenyl cation instability, (d) pi-cloud repulsion.\n8. Directing Effect in Haloarenes: Halogen is ORTHO/PARA directing (+R resonance effect), but DEACTIVATING (-I inductive effect dominates arenium stabilization).\n9. Grignard Anhydrous Mandate: RMgX reacts instantly with any active hydrogen compound (H2O, ROH, RNH2) to form alkane R-H. Even moisture destroys reagent.\n10. Williamson Ether Limitation: 3\u00b0 alkyl halide + alkoxide yields 100% alkene via E2 elimination; 1\u00b0 alkyl halide is required to obtain ether.",
